    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In June 1747, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> Afshar's death, a nine-day loya jirga near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> chose Ahmad Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> as Shah, and he renamed his Abdali tribe Durrani.</li><li>Ahmad Shah invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> eight times, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1757 and on 14 January 1761 decisively defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>, the high point of Afghan power.</li><li>His successors fought one another for the throne; by 1818 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> held little more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and its surroundings, and in 1823 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> displaced them.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Ahmad Shah's envoy Khwaja Mirhan reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1763 he refused to kowtow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>; the Qing read it as a claim of equality and cut ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, though the envoy himself was shown favour.</p></aside>
    </section>

