ng a twelve-kilometre front.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="162.1" x2="162.1" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="162.1" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="162.1" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><title>1762: Ahmad Shah crosses into Punjab for the sixth time, takes Amritsar, massacres thousands of Sikhs in the Vadda Ghallughara and destroys the Golden Temple.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="256.8" x2="256.8" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="256.8" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="256.8" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>1772: Ahmad Shah dies in the mountains east of Kandahar; after Shah Wali Khan is assassinated at Farah, Timur Shah takes the throne in November.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="607.4" x2="607.4" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="607.4" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="607.4" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>1809: On 7 June Shuja Shah signs the first Afghan pact with a European power, with the British East India Company; weeks later Mahmud Shah deposes him.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>1823: Ayub Shah, the last Sadozai ruler of the empire, is deposed and presumably killed.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1747 CE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">1823 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1747</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> Afshar's death in June, a loya jirga near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> lasting nine days chooses Ahmad Khan, crowned Padshah, Durr-i-Durran, by the holy man Sabir Khan.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1749</span><span class="rf-text">The Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> lands to Ahmad Shah to save his capital from attack.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1757</span><span class="rf-text">Ahmad Shah sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> but leaves the Mughal emperor in nominal control in return for recognising Afghan suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1761</span><span class="rf-text">On 14 January the Durranis decisively defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>, fought along a twelve-kilometre front.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text">Ahmad Shah crosses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> for the sixth time, takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, massacres thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> in the Vadda Ghallughara and destroys the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1772</span><span class="rf-text">Ahmad Shah dies in the mountains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>; after Shah Wali Khan is assassinated at Farah, Timur Shah takes the throne in November.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1809</span><span class="rf-text">On 7 June Shuja Shah signs the first Afghan pact with a European power, with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>; weeks later Mahmud Shah deposes him.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1823</span><span class="rf-text">Ayub Shah, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> ruler of the empire, is deposed and presumably killed.</span></li></ol>
