    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shah.jpg" alt="Nader Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Nader_Shah._Artist_unknown,_created_in_ca._1740_in_Iran_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Afghan independence had a forerunner: in 1709 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>, chief of the Ghilji tribe, broke from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a> ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1722 to 1725, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> Afshar toppled the Hotaks in 1738. Ahmad Khan was the son of Muhammad Zaman Khan, chief of the Abdali tribe, and a commander of Nader Shah. When Nader Shah died in June 1747, Ahmad secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, and a loya jirga, a grand council, met near Kandahar for nine days. Two chief contestants emerged, Hajji Jamal Khan of the Mohammadzai lineage and Ahmad Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a>. The holy man Mohammad Sabir Khan, who had predicted Ahmad's leadership, told the council that God had made him a greater man than any of them, placed sheaves of wheat or barley in his turban and crowned him Padshah, Durr-i-Durran, 'Pearl of the Pearls'. Ahmad renamed his tribe Durrani.</p><p>His first actions were to take Qalati Ghilji and Ghazni from the Ghilji and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> from the Mughal governor Nasir Khan. In 1749 the Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> lands to save his capital. Ahmad then turned west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, whose ruler Shahrukh Afshar acknowledged Afghan suzerainty, and sent an army north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> that brought the Tajik, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> and other peoples of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> under his control. The year 1747 marks the appearance of an Afghan state independent of both the Persian and Mughal empires.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lahore"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahore.jpg" alt="Lahore" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahore_Fort_view_from_Baradari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rohaan Bhatti, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire was a tribal state held together by its founder's management of Pashtun alliances and by the plunder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ahmad Shah's eight invasions brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> under Afghan rule, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, and the sack of Delhi in 1757 yielded the booty he coveted; the Mughal emperor was left in nominal control of his city as long as he acknowledged Afghan suzerainty south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. Ahmad was credited with balancing tribal alliances and hostilities to a remarkable degree and with directing tribal energies away from rebellion. That balance was later upset by his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a>, who replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> leaders with men of his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> lineage.</p><p>The empire's reach was fixed by negotiation as much as conquest. Rebellions in the north around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> led Ahmad Shah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Bukhara to agree that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> would divide their lands, and he may have sought a similar sphere of influence with the Qing, as he had with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> with him. In 1763 he occupied the lands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> at the plea of Fazil Biy of Kokand and Kyrgyz chieftains against Qing expansion, withdrawing by 1764 when no alliance formed, and sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> with a letter, probably a fath-nama celebrating Panipat, and gifts of four horses, painted by the Qing court painter Giuseppe Castiglione.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat.jpg" alt="Third Battle of Panipat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Third_battle_of_Panipat_13_January_1761.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Durrani army was built on cavalry armed with flintlocks, which made hit-and-run attacks combining new firearms with Turco-Mongol tactics. Its core was 10,000 mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a>, slave-soldiers carrying the sher-bacha blunderbuss, a third of them former Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>. The bulk of the army was irregular tribal cavalry armed with lance and broadsword; infantry and artillery played very small roles, apart from the zamburak, light swivel guns mounted on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. Ahmad Shah was famous for winning wars against much larger armies. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under Raghunathrao drove Timur Shah from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> in 1757, Ahmad declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, gathering warriors of many Afghan tribes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khan-i-of-kalat">Mir Nasir Khan I</a>, Khan of Kalat.</p><p>By 1759 his army had reached Lahore, and on 14 January 1761 it decisively defeated the Marathas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>, fought along a twelve-kilometre front between largely Muslim and largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> armies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> proved harder to subdue. In 1762 Ahmad crossed the passes for the sixth time, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, massacred thousands of Sikhs in the Vadda Ghallughara and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>; within two years the Sikhs rebelled again and rebuilt their holy city, and his later attempts to subjugate them failed. In 1768 Shah Wali Khan invaded Badakhshan and seized Fayzabad, and within the year Ahmad occupied the country and executed its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Shah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Shah ruled through the tribal order that had chosen him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> was the capital; under Timur Shah the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> as its winter seat. Succession was contested at every death. Ahmad named his son Timur Shah heir over the elder Sulaiman Shah, whom he thought violent and unpopular with the Kandahari Durranis; after his death in 1772 Shah Wali Khan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a> Jahan Khan concealed it, carried the body to Kandahar and proclaimed Sulaiman. Timur marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, Shah Wali and his sons were assassinated at Farah, Sulaiman surrendered, and Timur took the throne in November 1772.</p><p>When Timur died in 1793 three of his sons, governors of Kandahar, Herat and Kabul, contended for the throne; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a>, holding the capital, won at twenty-three and imprisoned many half-brothers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> efforts to impose an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> on the Pashtun tribes without the advice of the other tribal leaders failed. Zaman owed his throne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> chief Painda Khan but executed him and the chiefs of the Nurzai, Alizai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>; Painda Khan's son backed Zaman's brother Mahmud Shah, who took Kandahar without bloodshed, defeated Zaman in 1801 and had his eyes lanced. Mahmud ruled from 1801 to 1803 and 1809 to 1818, Shuja Shah from 1803 to 1809, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> briefly in 1810, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ali Shah in 1818–1819 and Ayub Shah from 1819 to 1823.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh.jpg" alt="Ranjit Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaj_Ranjit_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colonel James Skinner (1778-1841), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Durrani Empire is considered the foundational polity of the modern nation-state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and Ahmad Shah, remembered as Ahmad Shah Baba, its Father of the Nation. At its peak it ruled present-day Afghanistan, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, eastern Turkmenistan and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its victory at Panipat in 1761 broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> advance into north-western India, while the Mughal emperors became nominal heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> under Afghan suzerainty.</p><p>The empire also redrew its neighbours' ambitions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> became the agreed border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, and the Qing, after Ahmad's embassy of 1763 and his conquest of Badakhshan in 1768, abandoned the pretence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> were tributaries: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Shah of Badakhshan any aid and in effect recognised the Afghans as a rival power, gambling on the difficult terrain between the two realms for safety. The Durrani wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, including the massacre of the Vadda Ghallughara in 1762, were followed by the rebuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from the Afghans. The treaty Shuja Shah signed with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> on 7 June 1809, the first Afghan pact with a European power, pledged joint action against Franco-Persian aggression and foreshadowed the British restoration of Shuja in 1839 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani.jpg" alt="Zaman Shah Durrani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah Durrani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Zaman_Shah_Durrani_of_Afghanistan-cropped-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire began to unravel before its founder died. From 1762 his hold loosened: parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> were lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, and northern territories to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, forcing the compromise on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>. Ahmad Shah died in the mountains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in 1772. His successors governed so ineptly amid profound unrest that within fifty years the empire as such was at an end and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was in civil war. The quarrels among Timur Shah's sons after 1793 gave outside powers their pretext to intervene; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a>'s purge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> chiefs brought his overthrow and blinding in 1801, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, who had wrested power from Zaman's forces in Punjab, gave him asylum.</p><p>Zaman's fall opened a period of even greater violence: Mahmud Shah's first reign lasted two years before Shuja Shah replaced him; Shuja's treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in June 1809 was followed within weeks by his deposition; Mahmud's second reign ended when his brother deposed him in 1818; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ali Shah was strangled by his brother Isma'il, and the Durranis lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> at the Battle of Shopian in 1819. By 1818 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> controlled little more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and the territory within 160 kilometres of it, having alienated the other Durrani lineages. Ayub Shah was deposed and presumably killed in 1823, and Dost Muhammad Khan and the Barakzai dynasty deposed the Sadozai in Kabul, superseding the empire with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Afghanistan. A Sadozai line held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> until 1863.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Durrani Empire (1747–1823) was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> from the wreck of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> into a Pashtun state reaching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, victorious over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at Panipat in 1761 but unable to hold down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. Succession wars among his descendants left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> with little beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> displaced them.</em></p>

